# sen0414-course-build — own lesson deposit (v1.0.0), per the precedent recorded in
# backlog-roadmap-framework/06-package-provenance/registrant-deposit-snapshot/backlog_framework_lesson_deposit_v2_1_0.ttl
# (L-84: record lessons as they emerge; L-X7/B1: a consuming package mints ZERO OE-namespace
# subjects — these individuals are registrant-local, typed with the governed kb:LessonsLearned
# class, and relate to any future governed IRI only by rdfs:seeAlso once OEE, if it chooses,
# ratifies and mints one). Deposited here rather than written into any OE-owned knowledge_base
# ABox, because that package is owned by its own governance session.
#
# Duplicate-screened 2026-10-12 against oe-pack/09-governance/oegov_abox_v1_16_0.ttl (grep across
# ~5,000 lines for console/newline/grounding/ERD/semantic-rank/cutoff/mentions terms): no existing
# lesson addresses the CME materials-template rendering/checking pipeline these seven describe: it
# validates ontology and governance artifacts, not a page/deck renderer's own runtime formulas.
# All seven arose empirically, from real build-and-test cycles of SEN0414 chapters 8-12 against the
# shared CME-standard page/deck tooling (08-tooling/ in altunelyusuf/AdvancedProgrammingWithPython),
# a tooling line reused by every future chapter and, per the CME materials standard, by other
# courses built on the same template series — so each is a generalizable finding, not a SEN0414
# content fact, and belongs in a knowledge base rather than only in this course's own build commits.

pkg:CandidateLesson_DiagramGroundingIsFirstParagraphOnly a kb:LessonsLearned , owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-cand-sen0414-1: a narrative diagram's grounding check reads only a concept's first definition paragraph, its one-line ABox definition and its label — never its full multi-paragraph prose"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Observed repeatedly across SEN0414 chapters 8-12 (08-tooling/sen0414_chNN_diagrams_v*.py): each diagram module's own grounding() function concatenates PARAS()[0]['text'] (the first paragraph only), the ABox one-line definition, and the label, then matches diagram-label words against that text by plain substring containment ('w in text', not whole-word). A diagram authored against a concept's full, later-written multi-paragraph essay scores low or fails grounding even when every word it uses is true of the concept, because the essay's supporting detail lives in paragraphs 2+, outside the window the checker reads."@en ;
    skos:definition "When a generated artifact (here, a narrative diagram) is checked for being 'grounded in' a richer source text (here, a concept's full multi-paragraph definition), the checker may deliberately or incidentally read only a small, fixed slice of that source — commonly just its first paragraph plus a short formal definition — rather than the whole text a human author sees. An author who writes against the full source, not the checker's actual input window, produces artifacts that read as true but fail an automated grounding gate for a reason invisible from the source alone. Discipline: before authoring content meant to pass a grounding/substantiation check, read the checker's own extraction function to learn its exact input window (which paragraph, which fields, whole-word or substring matching), and write economically within that window rather than against the full source; treat the checker's extraction logic as part of the content's specification, not an implementation detail. Detection signal: a diagram, summary or caption that is factually accurate against the full source but still scores low on an automated grounding check." ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-10-12T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_YusufAltunel .

pkg:CandidateLesson_RowSourceTextMustStayNewlineFree a kb:LessonsLearned , owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-cand-sen0414-2: a rendered console row's SOURCE TEXT must contain no literal embedded newline, when the renderer draws one row as one paragraph and the checker re-parses by paragraph boundary"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Found authoring SEN0414 chapter 8's deck (08-tooling/ch08-deck/examples_v1_0_0.py): a console row holding a real embedded newline (a Python triple-quoted string with an actual line break, e.g. \"letter = '''Dear Alice,\\n\\nSee you soon,\\nBob'''\" written WITH a real break) rendered correctly by eye but broke deck_check_v1_0_0.py, which re-parses each slide's rows by XML <a:p> paragraph boundaries — one real newline in the row's text becomes two paragraphs, so the checker's re-execution of 'the row' only sees a fragment. The row still needs to EVALUATE to a multi-line string when exec'd/eval'd, so the fix is not to remove the newline but to keep the row's stored/displayed text newline-free while its evaluated VALUE still has one: write the literal in the Python source with a doubled backslash ('\\\\n' in the source, i.e. backslash-n as two characters), which Python re-interprets as the single escape '\\n' when the row is exec'd/eval'd, while the row's own source string contains no raw newline character for the renderer or checker to split on."@en ;
    skos:definition "When one logical row/cell/line of a generated artifact is rendered as exactly one paragraph or one line by a downstream renderer, and a separate checker re-parses that same artifact by the same paragraph/line boundary to verify it, the row's stored source text must never contain the literal character it is being segmented on — even when the row's EVALUATED value legitimately should contain that character. The general fix is to encode the character as an escape sequence in the stored source (so the source itself is free of the literal character) rather than removing the character from the evaluated result, provided the evaluation step (exec/eval/template substitution) correctly un-escapes it back. Detection signal: an artifact that displays correctly to a human but fails a downstream re-parse/re-execution check that segments by the same boundary the content secretly contains." ;
    rdfs:seeAlso kb:Lesson_L_84_TextAppendPrefixAndDelimiterHazards ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-10-12T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_YusufAltunel .

pkg:CandidateLesson_LayoutFitChecksAreExactFormulasNotEstimates a kb:LessonsLearned , owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-cand-sen0414-3: a layout/fit checker's rejection is a deterministic formula over fixed constants (per-font character width, line height, fixed frame geometry) — read the formula and compute the fix, never estimate it"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Observed across every SEN0414 chapter 8-12 deck build: deck_layout_check_v1_0_0.py estimates each text frame's wrapped height from fixed per-font width constants (Courier New 0.60, Cambria 0.50, Calibri 0.48, line height factor 1.22) and flags any frame whose estimate exceeds its given height by more than 0.5pt; slide titles are given fontSize 22 if their text exceeds 46 characters (else 25 or 28), against a fixed ~44.6pt title-box height, so any title in the 47+ character tier that would still wrap to two lines at size 22 (roughly beyond 53 characters) is rejected. deck_fit_check (run separately in build_v1_0_0.sh) additionally flags overlapping text-frame bounding boxes, including a fixed takeaway-strip band at a constant y-range on every content slide. Every one of the ~10 layout/fit violations hit while building these five decks was resolved by reading the exact formula/threshold that fired and computing the minimal title/text/width/font change algebraically from it, never by trial-and-error resizing."@en ;
    skos:definition "An automated layout or fit checker that rejects a generated visual artifact (a slide, a panel, a frame) is, underneath, evaluating an exact formula against fixed constants and fixed geometry — not a fuzzy aesthetic judgment. Treating a rejection as something to fix by iteratively nudging sizes is slower and less reliable than reading the checker's own source for its exact formula (which font-width table, which line-height factor, which fixed frame height or y-range) and solving for the adjustment algebraically. The same discipline generalizes to any deterministic checker whose pass/fail is computed from a readable formula: read the formula before attempting a fix. Detection signal: more than one trial-and-error resize attempt against the same checker failure." ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-10-12T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_YusufAltunel .

pkg:CandidateLesson_TwoColumnLayoutSwitchesOnceNeverRebalances a kb:LessonsLearned , owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-cand-sen0414-4: a two-column flowing layout renderer that overflows column 0 switches to column 1 exactly once and never re-balances afterward — the author must size the groups, not rely on the renderer"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Found in 08-tooling/deck_render_v1_0_0.js's drawLinks(), used by every SEN0414 chapter's resources slide: resource-link groups are drawn top-to-bottom in column 0 until a group's own height would overflow the panel, at which point rendering switches to column 1 ONE TIME and continues there for every remaining group, with no re-balancing check — so column 1 can itself overflow if the groups placed into it are collectively too large, even though column 0 has spare room. Hit and fixed on chapters 8-12's resources slides by manually sizing/splitting the resource groups so their row counts balance across the two columns, rather than trusting the renderer to balance them."@en ;
    skos:definition "A rendering routine that distributes a flowing sequence of variable-sized groups across a fixed number of columns by a simple 'switch to the next column when this one would overflow' rule performs a one-way, one-time decision per column boundary: once content starts flowing into column N+1, the routine does not reconsider whether an earlier column still has room or whether column N+1 itself will overflow from the remaining groups. Content authored against such a renderer must pre-balance the groups' sizes across the known number of columns before render time; the renderer's own overflow switch is a safety valve for a single transition, not a load-balancing algorithm. Detection signal: a later column overflowing its panel while an earlier column has visible spare space." ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-10-12T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_YusufAltunel .

pkg:CandidateLesson_RelationshipGraphTestsNeedDeliberateCrossReferences a kb:LessonsLearned , owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-cand-sen0414-5: an automated relationship-graph (ERD) test that probes a fixed Nth node in document order requires that node to carry a real cross-reference — content authored concept-by-concept, in isolation, produces isolated nodes that are individually correct but fail such a test"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Found authoring SEN0414 chapter 8's document prose: the page's section-level ERD draws one entity per level-2 subject with edges derived from an auto-computed 'mentions' relation (one level-3 concept's exact label text appearing, case-insensitively with word boundaries, inside another level-3 concept's own paragraph text, rolled up to their level-2 parents). The shared Playwright suite's ERD interaction tests click the 3rd entity in document order and require it to have at least one such relationship. Chapter 8's freshly authored prose, written one concept at a time without deliberate cross-references, left that 3rd section with zero relationships — confirmed by writing a standalone census script comparing every level-2 section's mentions against the test's own selector logic — even though every individual section's content was accurate. Fixed with two short, accurate added sentences naming other sections' exact concept labels, in a newly versioned document file so the already-hashed prior version stayed untouched."@en ;
    skos:definition "When a derived relationship graph's edges are computed automatically from textual co-mention (one entity's name appearing inside another's description) rather than asserted directly, an author who writes each entity's description in isolation — true and complete on its own — can still leave that entity with zero derived edges, simply because nothing else happens to name it and it happens to name nothing else. A test that exercises 'a node with at least one relationship', selected by a fixed position in document order, will then fail on correct content. The content defect is real but easy to misdiagnose as a test or renderer bug: the fix is to deliberately include a small number of natural, accurate cross-references between sibling entities' descriptions during authoring (not after test failure), checked early with a standalone connectivity census against the graph's own derivation rule, rather than reactively patching the one node a test happens to probe. Detection signal: a graph-interaction test failing with 'node has no relationships' against content that reads as factually complete and correct." ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-10-12T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_YusufAltunel .

pkg:CandidateLesson_EmbeddingRankCutoffFailsShortQueriesOnNoise a kb:LessonsLearned , owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-cand-sen0414-6: a passage-search widget that ranks by embedding similarity under a fixed top-K cutoff can place a short query's true match just outside the cutoff from embedding noise alone, with no content defect"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Found authoring SEN0414 chapter 10's page: the shared page-wide passage search (#srchmore/#srchpass) ranks candidate passages by MiniLM semantic similarity, keeps only the top 8, and applies a literal-token filter after that cutoff. A generic Playwright test selects the 4th level>=2 node in document order and searches its single longest word; for chapter 10, that word ('flavours', for the 'Path flavours' section) landed at semantic rank 8 for the single-word query — one place past the cutoff — purely from embedding noise, confirmed directly by scripting a live browser session to read the actual rank. The content was correct; the word simply did not embed close enough to its own section, among the corpus's other passages, to clear a fixed top-8 window on a one-word query. Fixed without touching the shared test or template by reordering doc:sectionOrder (no prose change) so a different, safely-ranking word's section landed in that test's probed slot."@en ;
    skos:definition "A semantic (embedding-similarity) search ranking under a fixed top-K cutoff is not guaranteed to rank a passage's own best single-word self-description inside that cutoff, especially for short queries, because embedding similarity reflects corpus-wide semantic neighborhoods rather than lexical identity: a correct passage can simply have several other passages that embed closer to a given single word than it does to its own defining word. A test harness that always probes 'the Nth item's longest word' inherits this fragility for every item that happens to land in that probed position, independent of whether that item's content is correct. Where the test and template are shared and out of scope to change, the available fix is positional: reorder which content occupies the probed position (a metadata/ordering change) rather than rewrite correct content to chase an embedding ranking. Detection signal: a single-word search test failing intermittently or for specific positions while the same search behaves correctly for longer, multi-word queries on the same content; confirm the actual rank by scripting the search live before concluding content is at fault." ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-10-12T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_YusufAltunel .

pkg:CandidateLesson_SharedTestConfigDefaultsMustBeExplicitlyOverridden a kb:LessonsLearned , owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "L-cand-sen0414-7: a shared per-unit test configuration's feature-switch defaults are not the right defaults for a real consumer — check every sibling consumer's own override set before shipping a new configuration, not after test failures"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Found building SEN0414 chapter 8's test configuration: sen0414_page_test_v9_28_0.py's hardcoded default CFG dict sets corpus_kinds to only ['chapter','page','research'], course_outcomes to false and code_layers to false, but every one of the seven chapters shipped before chapter 8 (confirmed by grepping all seven test_config files) overrides all three: corpus_kinds to the full ['book','chapter','course','page','research'], course_outcomes to true, and code_layers to true whenever the chapter has real executed programs with AST trees. Chapter 8's first-shipped test_config omitted these overrides (inherited the checker's bare defaults), producing 2 of that chapter's first 4 Playwright failures — not because the content was wrong, but because the configuration under-declared what the already-correct content actually provided. Every chapter built afterward added the full override set from the start, avoiding the same discovery cycle."@en ;
    skos:definition "When many structurally similar units (here, course chapters) each ship their own instance of a shared configuration file, and that shared checker's own hardcoded defaults are conservative placeholders rather than the real, intended per-unit settings, a newly authored configuration that simply omits an override - trusting the checker's bare default - will fail checks that the unit's actual content already satisfies. The default is not wrong in isolation; it is simply not what any real, mature consumer actually wants, and every one of them already overrides it. Before shipping a new unit's first configuration, diff it against every already-shipped sibling's configuration for the full set of overridden switches, and carry the same override set forward by default rather than discovering each omission reactively through its own test failure." ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <http://example.org/knowledge-base> ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-10-12T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo oe-prov:Agent_YusufAltunel .
